KEYWORDS. 2D covalent organic polymers, metal organic frameworks, nanoconfinement, oxygen reduction reaction, electrocatalysis
## ABSTRACT
The output energy capacity of green electrochemical devices, e.g., fuel cells, depends strongly on the sluggish oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), which requires catalysts. One of the desired features for highly efficient ORR electrocatalytic materials is the rich of well-defined activate sites. Herein, we developed a facile approach to prepare highly efficient non-precious metal and nitrogen-doped carbon-based ORR catalysts basing on covalent organic polymers (COPs) synthesized
in-situ in the nanoconfined space of highly order metal organic frameworks (MOFs). The MOF templet ensured the developed electrocatalysts to possess a high surface area with homogeneously distributed small metal/nitrogen active sites, as confirmed by X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) measurements and first-principles calculations, leading to highly efficient ORR electrocatalytic activity. Notably, the developed COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 exhibits 16 mV positive half-wave potential comparing with the benchmarked Pt/C.
Fuel cell has been regarded as a promising technology to meet the energy requirements for future electric vehicles and/or as stationary power sources.\(^{1-4}\) However, the performance of fuel cells depends strongly on the sluggish oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) at the cathode.\(^{2,5}\) Although certain precious metals (e.g., Pt-based catalysts) have achieved unbeatable electrocatalytic activities for ORR, they are still subjected to multiple disadvantages, including their high cost, scarcity, limited stability, and fast deactivation by CO, which still hampered fuel cells for practical applications.\(^{6-8}\) Therefore, it is critical to develop efficient and durable ORR electrocatalysts at low cost.\(^{9-11}\) Recently, carbon supported transition metal-nitrogen complexes have been recognized as one of promising candidates for substituting Pt-based electrocatalysts for ORR.\(^{12-16}\) Although the active sites in these nitrogen-rich transition metal/nanocarbon catalysts have not been understood clearly, pyolinic-type and graphitic-type nitrogen sites in a nanocarbon matrix and transition metal-related compounds (e.g., FeN, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}\), and CoO) have been generally considered as effective active sites for the eletrocatalysis of oxygen reduction to hydroxyl (OH\(^-\)) in alkaline solution.\(^{11,17,18}\) Meanwhile, Fe (or Co)-\(\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{x}}\) species in a nanocarbon matrix are favorite active sites for ORR in acidic solution.\(^{18-20}\) More recently, highly efficient ORR catalysts have also been prepared through carbonization of precursor polymers containing metal-porphyrin moieties.\(^{21-23}\)
Covalent organic polymers (COPs)\(^{24-30}\) are a class of multidimensional and multifunctional porous organic networks consisting of covalent bonds (B-O, C-C, C-H, C-N et al.) between organic linkers. We have recently employed metal porphyrin
as monomer to produce well-defined 2D porphyrin-based COPs, which, after carbonization, exhibited good electrocatalytic activities toward 4e oxygen reduction in both alkaline and acid media with an excellent stability and free from any methanol-crossover/CO-poisoning effect.\(^{21}\) During carbonization, however, the pyrolyzed COP (COP-P) showed a strong stacking effect, which leads to the aggregation of metal species (particle size \(>30\ \mathrm{nm}\)) and subsequently reduces density of active sites and limits electrocatalytic activity. To further enhance the ORR electrocatalytic activity, it is critical to produce small particles with uniform distribution.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructing of coordinate bond between metal ions and organic linkers, are a class of tunable porous materials with a high surface area and free volume.\(^{31-34}\) The coordinated bond in MOFs can be easily removed in acid media to generate well-defined voids. In this study, we used a well-defined MOF-180\(^{35}\) with an ultrahigh free volume as a template of nano-confined space for in situ growing well-defined COPs. By replicating the MOF-180 template, the resultant COPs possess a high electrocatalytic surface area, containing uniform small metal nanoparticles (\(<10\ \mathrm{nm}\)) and homogeneously distributed metal/nitrogen active sites with a relatively small size to show a highly efficient ORR electrocatalytic activity. Comparing to the sample synthesized free from the nanoconfinement template, the developed COP electrocatalysts exhibited a \(58\ \mathrm{mV}\) reduction in the half-wave potential and \(17.5\%\) increase in the limiting current, and a remarkable durability and good methanol tolerance.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Figure 1 shows the synthetic route for preparing the COP materials within the confined space of MOFs. COP-TPP was synthesized with a Scholl reaction involving the elimination of two aryl-bound hydrogen atoms of Meso-Tetraphenyl- porphyrin in the presence of cheap Friedel-Crafs catalyst.36 MOF-180 with a high porosity and high specific surface area was prepared and employed as a template, which allowed COP polymerization to occur within its confined space (Figure S1).35,37 Thereafter, the MOF-180 was removed by acid washing to produce COP materials via the confined reaction (Termed as COP-TPP@MOF). For comparison, we have also prepared COP-TPP without confinement (termed as COP-TPP). Solid state \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) CP/MAS NMR spectra and elemental analysis of COPs were performed to prove the successful C-C coupling (Figure S2 and Table S1). Both COP-TPP@MOF and COP-TPP showed similar contents of carbon, nitrogen, and hydrogen elements (Table S1). Furthermore, the ICP measurements revealed a negligible Zn content in the as-synthesized COP-TPP@MOF (0.0976%), suggesting a complete removal of the MOF- 180 templet (Table S2). Clearly, the macro-stacking effect on COP-TPP has been remarkably reduced by using MOF-180 as the confined space template (Figure 2a), comparing with severely stacked morphology for the template-free COP-TPP (Figure 2d). Moreover, COP-TPP@MOF showed a more porous texture with respect to COP-TPP because of the use of the MOF-180 templet with a high porosity. The BET specific surface area of COP-TPP also increased from 790 to \(955\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) by growing in the MOF template (Table S3).
The as-synthesized COP-TPP@MOF was further incorporated with metal (Fe and/or Co), followed by carbonization to improve its electrocatalytic activity. The prepared electrocatalysts were termed as COP-TPP@MOF (Fe and/or Co)-X (X refers to the carbonization temperature). The reference samples were also prepared free from the MOF templet and designated as COP-TPP (Fe and/or Co)-X (X refers to the carbonization temperature). Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images revealed that COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 contain more uniform and much smaller (<15 nm) iorn particles than those Fe particles in COP-TPP(Fe)-900 (Figure 2b-2c and 2e-2f). Metal particles are encapsulated with multiple graphitic carbon layers, forming highly active and stable architecture in both COP-TPP(Fe)-900 and COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 (Figures 2c, 2f and S3).
The structure and composition of COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-X are further characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) and Raman spectroscopy. There are two pronounced peaks at \(26^{\circ}\) and \(43^{\circ}\) in XRD pattern (Figure S4b), which can be attributed to the (002) and (101) planes of the graphite carbon in COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900, respectively.\(^{38,39}\) There is no obviously detectable iron related peak in the XRD pattern due to the low iron content (ICP results show 1.269 wt%; Table S4) or disordered iron species formed in COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900. The Raman spectra given in Figure S4a show typical D and G bands at 1349 and 1588 cm\(^{-1}\) designated to disordered and graphitic phases, respectively, after carbonized at 800 °C, 900 °C, 1000 °C.\(^{17}\) The relatively high intensity of the D band over the G band is attributable to the long-range disorder associated with those interdispersed
small holes of rich edge defects.\(^{40}\) The relatively strong G band with respect to the single-peak 2D band indicates a multilayered graphitic structure of COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-X.\(^{30}\)
To evaluate the electrocatalytic activities for ORR, we first carried out cyclic voltammetry (CV) and linear sweep voltammetric (LSV) measurements in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated 0.1 M KOH solution at room temperature. By so doing, we investigated the effect carbonized temperature and metal species on the ORR activity for COP-TPP(M)@MOF-900. It was found that COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 carbonized under \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) exhibited the best ORR performance (Figure S6). Apparently, MOF-900, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 and COP-TPP(Fe)-900 catalysts all showed well-defined cathodic ORR current peaks (Figure 3a). COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 exhibited a more positive peak potential at 0.84 V versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) compared to MOF-900 (0.59 V versus RHE) and COP-TPP(Fe)-900 (0.77 V versus RHE). To further investigate the ORR performance, we carried out the LSV measurements on a rotating disk electrode (RDE, Figure 3b). Similar to the CV curves, the sample prepared under nanoconfinement, i.e., COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900, has the highest electrocatalytic activity among all the samples studied in terms of the onset potential, half-wave potential and limiting current. The nanoconfinement templet (MOF-900) itself exhibited a low electrocatalytic performance with a half-wave potential at only 0.65 V versus RHE. Compared to COP-TPP(Fe)-900, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 showed a more positive onset potential (0.99V vs RHE), about \(58\;\mathrm{mV}\) increase in the half-wave potential and \(17.5\%\) enlargement for limiting
current. Furthermore, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 exhibited a more \(16~\mathrm{mV}\) positive half-wave potential than the commercial Pt/C.
The excellent ORR activity of COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 was also verified by the Tafel slope and electron transfer number. As shown in Figure 3c, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 has a smaller Tafel slope \((68~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1})\) than that of MOF-900 \((83~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1})\) and COP-TPP(Fe)-900 \((76~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1})\), but comparable to that of Pt/C \((66~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1})\). Based on the RRDE results in the potential range of 0 to \(0.8~\mathrm{V}\) (Figure S6), we can calculate the electron transfer number \((n)\). Among them, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 shows the highest \(n\) up to 3.95 at \(0.6~\mathrm{V}\), which is even higher than that of Pt/C (3.91) (Figure 3d), indicating a four-electron pathway for ORR on the COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 surface to form water as the main product (Figure S6). This suggests that COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 has similar catalytic mechanism for ORR as Pt/C, where the adsorption of oxygen molecular is probably the rate-determining step\(^{41}\) (see following calculations for COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900). Therefore, these results indicate that the nanoconfinement-synthesis approach is efficient for enhancing the ORR performance of electrocatalysts.
To further investigate the long-term stability of COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900, we compared LSV curves before and after several thousand cycles and studied methanol tolerance by chronoamperometric response \((i{-}t)\), respectively. Figure 3e shows that COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 retained similar LSV curves even after 5000 continuous cycles between 0.59 and \(0.99~\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) at a scan rate of \(100~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}\) in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated \(0.1~\mathrm{M}\) KOH. Furthermore, electrochemical accessible surface area (EASA) is also an
important factor to evaluate the ORR activity. Figure S8 presents the CVs between 1.2 and 1.0 V vs. RHE at various scan rates for COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 catalyst in argon saturated 0.1M KOH. Consistent with the trend observed with BET surface areas, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 showed the highest EASA of \(316\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) in alkaline, which are higher than COP-TPP(Fe)-900 \((247\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) (Figure 3f). The increased EASA can partially explain the enhanced ORR activity of COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900. Furthermore, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 also shows strong methanol tolerance with only about \(6\%\) current decrease upon the addition of methanol in chronoamperometric response (Figure S9), comparing with about \(40\%\) decrease for the commercialized Pt/C under the same condition.
In addition, recent theoretical and experimental studies suggest Fe and/or Co-doping on carbon materials are favorite for highly efficient ORR catalysis. 12,22,42-44 In this context, we also further synthesized different metal incorporated COP derived carbon, i.e., COP-TPP(M)-900 (M=Fe and/or Co) using MOF-180 as nanoconfinement. As clearly shown in Figure 4a, Fe played an important role in the ORR activity of COP-TPP(M)@MOF-900 in respect to its counterparts with Co or Fe/Co mixture, while Fe/Co codoped COP derived carbon exhibited better electrocatalytic performance than Co-doped COP derived carbon. To gain further insights into the ORR pathway and active sites, we performed the first-principle calculations to understand the charge density distribution and the free energy of each intermediates on the surface. In view of the demonstrated good correlation between the COP precursor structure and the resultant COP derived carbon, we carried out
density function theory (DFT) calculations of the model COP precursors (Figure S10) to understand the inherent electronic properties.\(^{30}\)
First, the lattice optimization image and the 3D charge density distribution of COP-TPP, COP-TPP(Co)@MOF and COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF are shown in Figure S9 and Figure S11, respectively. Based on these calculations, 2D charge density distribution (Figure 4b and 4c) can be obtained and reveal that the introduction of metal atoms improves the positive charge density, which is localized on the metal-Nx coordination sites. As clearly shown in 3D charge density distribution (Figure 4d), the negative charge density is anchored at carbon atoms, while positive charge density is seated at metal-Nx coordination sites. Therefore, these results suggest Metal-Nx is the prior sites for oxygen molecular adsorption on COP derived carbon surface, as emphasized in many previous studies.\(^{45}\) According to the above results, ORR on COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 goes through a four-electron process.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopic (XPS) analysis indicates the presence of C, N, Fe and O elements (Figure S5). As shown in Figure 5c, the high-resolution N1s spectrum was fitted with four different signals having binding energies of 398.9, 399.6, 401.3, and 402.0 eV, which can be assigned to pyridinic N, Fe−Nx moiety, graphitic N, and oxidized N, respectively.\(^{11,46}\) The apparent peak at around 399.6 eV
confirms the presence of \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{x}}\) moiety in COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900, which agrees with EXAFS results. The amount of nitrogen corresponding to \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{x}}\) structures was about 28 at.%, while the pyridinic N reached close to 10 at.% of total doped nitrogen, which might serve as metal coordination sites to coordinate with Fe to form \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{x}}\) active center for ORR due to their lone-pair electrons.\(^{49,50}\) Graphitic N was about 40 at% in total doped nitrogen, which also has been reported to play a crucial role in ORR.\(^{51,52}\) Overall, all the three kinds of nitrogen species could contribute to the observed high catalytic activity and stability for the COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 electrocatalyst.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we have developed a simple approach to highly efficient non-precious metal and nitrogen-doped carbon ORR catalysts based on covalent organic polymers (COPs) synthesized in nano-confined space of order highly porous MOF materials as templet. Highly crystalline MOF-180 was used as template to restrict the growth of COP materials. The nanoconfinement leads to a high BET surface area, homogeneous distribution and small size of numerous metal/nitrogen active sites with well-defined mesoporous structures attractive for electrocatalysis. Results from both the first principle calculations and experimental measurements suggest that the Fe incorporated COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 is the best among the Fe and/or Co incorporated samples, which shows highly efficient ORR electrocatalytic activity in alkaline media. Notably, the developed COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900
electrocatalyst exhibits a high ORR activity with an onset potential of \(0.99~\mathrm{V}\) (versus RHE) and a half-wave potential of \(0.85~\mathrm{V}\) (versus RHE), which exhibits a more \(16~\mathrm{mV}\) positive half-wave potential than the benchmark Pt/C. Moreover, COP-TPP(Fe)@MOF-900 exhibits remarkable durability and good methanol tolerance throughout electrochemical operation. In short, the nano-confinement-induced method using highly crystalline templet is a feasible and important approach to high performance electrocatalysts, and the developed catalysts hold promising potential as efficient ORR catalysts for fuel cells or metal air batteries.
## MATERIALS AND METHODS
Synthesis of COP-TPP@MOF. In a glove box, the activated MOF-180 (0.12 g) and monomer meso-Tetraphenylporphine (0.12 g, 0.2 mmol) were subjected to stirring in the solvent for \(3~\mathrm{h}\) to allow the monomer to be incorporated in the cavity of MOF-180. Then, small-molecule \(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}\) (0.5 g, 3.2 mmol) was added to the react for \(48~\mathrm{h}\) at \(58~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The MOF templet and \(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}\) can be well removed by the subsequent HCl treatment.
Synthesis of COP-TPP (M=Fe and/or Co) @MOF (Fe): The as-synthesized COP-TPP @MOF (0.08 g) and \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}\) (0.4 g) or \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) (0.4 g) or \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}\) (0.2 g) & \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) (0.2 g) were dissolved in \(5~\mathrm{ml}\) DMF with magnetic stirring at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\), the resulting precipitate was then washed with ethanol and deionized water, and dried in a vacuum oven at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\).
Synthesis of COP-TPP(M)@MOF-X. The obtained COP-TPP(M)@MOF was then carbonized at various temperatures to obtain COP-TPP(M)@MOF-X (X represents
carbonization temperature of 800, 900, and \(1000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). COP-TPP(M)@MOF powder was first heated at a heating rate of \(6\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C\min}^{-1}\) up to \(350\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\), and then at a heating rate of \(6\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C\min}^{-1}\) up to 800/900/1000 \({}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\). Then, COP-TPP(M)@MOF-X catalysts were added into \(0.5\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) string at \(80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(8\ \mathrm{h}\) to remove inactive substance on surface of catalysts.
Physical Characterization. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) measurements were performed with D/MAX 2000 X-ray diffractometer with Cu Kα line \((\lambda=1.54178\ \mathrm{\AA})\) as the incident beam. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was performed on ThermoVG ESCALAB 250. BET data were gained on ASAP 2020 V3.04 H. FT-IR spectroscopy was performed on an VERTEX 70V instrument with the wavenumber range of \(400{-}4000\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). Scanning electron microscopic (SEM) images were obtained on a S4700 SEM instrument. Transmission electron microscopic (TEM) images were obtained on an H800 instrument. Elemental analysis (C, H, N and S) was performed on a Thermo Fisher scientific Elemental Analyzer (varionELcube). Raman spectra were collected by the Raman Spectroscopy (Renishaw), using \(514\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser. Solid-state NMR spectra were measured on a Bruker AV300 spectrometer operating at \(75.5\ \mathrm{MHz}\) for \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\). EXAFS measurement: X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) measurement was performed on the 1W1B-X-ray absorption spectrum experiment stations of Beijing Synchrotron Radiation Facility. The EXAFS spectra of the Fe catalysts were recorded in fluorescence mode using a Lytle detector with a Ga filter. Data reduction was analyzed using the program Athena. The k3 weighted extended EXAFS oscillation in the range of \(1.5{-}13.6\ \mathrm{\AA}^{-1}\) was Fourier-transformed (FT).
Electrochemical evluation. All the electrochemical measurements were carried out in a conventional three-electrode cell using CHI760E electrochemical workstation controlled equipped with a glassy carbon rotating disk electrode (RDE) at room temperature. Glassy carbon (GC) electrodes (5.61mm diameter, PINE instrument Inc.) were polished with a 0.5, 0.15 and \(0.05~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) alumina slurry (CH Instrument Inc.) in turn and subsequently rinsed with ultrapure water and ethanol.
Prepare the electrode. \(5~\mathrm{mg}\) of electrocatalyst was ultrasonically dispersed in \(50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of Nafion solution \((0.5\;\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) and \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of anhydrous ethanol for about 30 minutes to form a homogeneous ink. Then, \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the as-prepared catalyst ink (containing \(50~\upmu\mathrm{g}\) of catalyst) was dropped on a glassy carbon rotating disk electrode giving a loading of \(0.2\;\mathrm{mg\;cm}^{-2}\). The commercially carbon-supported \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) electrode was prepared according to the above procedure with loading of \(\sim0.2\;\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-2}\).
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements (using a CHI 760E) were conducted in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}\) electrolyte using saturated calomel electrode (SCE, 0.99 vs. RHE in \(0.1\,\mathrm{~M~KOH}\)) as the reference electrode, \(0.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) platinum foil as the counter electrode and the electrocatalyst modified glassy carbon electrode as the working electrode. Electrolyte \((0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH})\) was saturated with high-purity oxygen at room temperature by bubbling \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) prior to the measurements for at least 30 mins. A flow of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was maintained over the electrolyte during the recording of CVs. The working electrode was cycled at least 25 times before the datas were recorded at a scan rate \(100\;\mathrm{mV\cdot s}^{-1}\). In control experiments, CV measurements were performed in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) saturated electrolyte.
Rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) measurements. The disk electrode was scanned cathodically at a rate of \(5\;\mathrm{mV\cdot s^{-1}}\) and the ring potential was constant at \(1.5\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE. The electron transfer number (n) was determined by the followed equation:
\[
n=4\times\frac{I_d}{I_d+I_r/N}
\]
where \(I_{d}\) is disk current, \(I_{r}\) is ring current and \(N=0.37\) is the current collection efficiency of the Pt ring. The peroxide percentage (\% \(\mathrm{HO}_{2}\)) was calculated based on the equation:
\[
\%HO_2^-=200\times\frac{I_r/N}{I_d+I_r/N}
\]
Electrochemically accessible surface area (EASA) measurements. EASA values were obtained by measuring electrical double layer capacitance (Cdl) between 1.2 and 1 V vs. RHE CVs in argon saturated 0.1M KOH electrolyte at various scan rates, where no Faradaic processes were observed to occur\(^{53}\). The current measured by the CV reflects the non-Faradaic capacitance associated with EASA on these catalysts. The current is denoted as double-layer charging current (Icapacitive), which is proportional to the scan rate (v). Thus, the gravimetric double layer capacitance Cdl (\(\mathrm{F\;g^{-1}}\)) at a given scan rate (v) and mass of catalyst deposited on the electrode (m) can be related to the Icapacitive as shown by \(\mathrm{SQ}3^{54}\).
\[
\mathrm{Cdl}=\mathrm{I}/\mathrm{vm}
\]
(SQ3)
EASA defined in SQ4 was then obtained using the estimated Cdl values.
\[
\mathrm{EASA=Cdl/Cs}
\]
(SQ4)
For calculations, we used the double layer capacitance (\(\mathrm{F\;m^{-2}}\)) of the glassy
carbon electrode surface (Cs) value of \(0.2\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) based on literature.
